.TH ARCHIVECTL 1 2026-11-15 "archivectl" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
archivectl \- numbered, encrypted Borg archives of a directory, here or on another host
.SH SYNOPSIS
.SY archivectl
[\fB\-C\fR\~\fI<dir>\fR]
[\fB\-h\fR]
\fICOMMAND\fR [\fIARG\fR\~.\|.\|.]
.YS
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B archivectl
keeps numbered, encrypted, deduplicated archives of a directory in a
Borg repository, on this host or on another one.
.B init
makes the directory a backup source and keeps the repository's
passphrase in
.BR pass (1);
.B create
archives it;
.BR list ,
.B diff
and
.B restore
show what the archives hold and bring it back; and
.B delete
removes the archives you name.
An archive's id is its number, never given twice.
Records go to standard output, one per line, tab-separated; messages to
standard error.
Every command that writes takes
.B \-n
to only report what it would do.
Run it in the backed-up directory or below it, or name that with
.BR \-C .
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
archivectl init && archivectl create
archivectl diff 4
archivectl restore \-\-to /tmp/old 4 notes.txt
archivectl list \-\-older 30 | archivectl delete \-n
archivectl delete 2 3
archivectl init \-\-repo ssh://backup\-host/srv/borg/home__adhd__test
.EE
.SH COMMANDS
.SS "archivectl init [\-n] [\-\-repo <url>] [\-\-no\-pass] [\-\-store\-passphrase] [<path>]"
.PP
Make <path>, or the current directory, a backup source with an encrypted Borg repository under \%$BORG_BACKUP_ROOT, or at <url>, and keep its passphrase in pass. initialise is an alias.
.PD 0
.TP 20n
\fB\-n\fR, \fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
Report what would be written and write nothing.
.TP 20n
\fB\-\-repo\fR <url>
Use the repository at <url>, ssh:// or user@host:path.
.TP 20n
\fB\-\-no\-pass\fR
Keep the passphrase out of pass.
.TP 20n
\fB\-\-store\-passphrase\fR
Keep the passphrase of an older source in pass.
.PD
.SS "archivectl create [\-n] [\-m <text>] [\-\-stats]"
.PP
Archive the source into a new archive and print its record. A backup source in a parent directory is then archived too.
.PD 0
.TP 22n
\fB\-n\fR, \fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
List on standard error what would be archived.
.TP 22n
\fB\-m\fR, \fB\-\-message\fR <text>
Keep <text> with the archive as its comment.
.TP 22n
\fB\-\-stats\fR
Also print the statistics borg gives.
.PD
.SS "archivectl status"
.PP
Print one record of the backup source here: source, repository, number of archives, number and date of the newest, and the bytes it takes.
.SS "archivectl list [\-\-human\-readable] [\-\-older N] [\-\-newer N] [\-\-first N] [\-\-last N]"
.PP
One record per archive, oldest first: number, date, size, files, the size deleting it frees, and its comment. Age options select, then \-\-first, \-\-last.
.PD 0
.TP 18n
\fB\-\-human\-readable\fR
Sizes in K, M, G, T instead of bytes.
.TP 18n
\fB\-\-older\fR N
Only the archives older than N days.
.TP 18n
\fB\-\-newer\fR N
Only the archives newer than N days.
.TP 18n
\fB\-\-first\fR N
Then only the oldest N.
.TP 18n
\fB\-\-last\fR N
Then only the newest N.
.PD
.SS "archivectl delete [\-n] [<id>...]"
.PP
Delete the archives given by number or name, or else those whose ids begin the lines on standard input, as list prints them. Ids and piped input together are refused. borg compact frees the space.
.PD 0
.TP 15n
\fB\-n\fR, \fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
Report what would be deleted and delete nothing.
.PD
.SS "archivectl restore [\-n] [\-\-to <dir> | \-\-mount <dir>] <id> [<path>...]"
.PP
Extract archive <id>, or the given paths in it, into the source, but no .archivectl file. A mount needs FUSE; borg umount <dir> unmounts it.
.PD 0
.TP 15n
\fB\-n\fR, \fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
Report what would be done and write nothing.
.TP 15n
\fB\-\-to\fR <dir>
Extract into <dir> instead.
.TP 15n
\fB\-\-mount\fR <dir>
Mount the archive at <dir> instead.
.PD
.SS "archivectl diff [\-\-scratch <dir>] <id> [<id2>]"
.PP
Print one record per path that differs from archive <id> to <id2>, or to the live source: the path, then added, removed or modified. Files are compared by content; for the live source the archive is first extracted into $TMPDIR, or /tmp, and needs room there.
.PD 0
.TP 17n
\fB\-\-scratch\fR <dir>
Extract into <dir> instead.
.PD
.SS "archivectl check"
.PP
Check the repository with borg check; the status is borg's.
.SS "archivectl export\-key [\-\-paper]"
.PP
Print the key of the source's repository, for a copy kept away from it.
.PD 0
.TP 9n
\fB\-\-paper\fR
The form borg gives for typing the key back by hand.
.PD
.SS "archivectl change\-passphrase [\-n]"
.PP
Give the repository a new passphrase, typed twice or \%BORG_NEW_PASSPHRASE, in the pass entry the configuration reads too.
.PD 0
.TP 15n
\fB\-n\fR, \fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
Report what would change and change nothing.
.PD
.SH COMMON OPTIONS
.PD 0
.TP 12n
\fB\-C\fR\~\fI<dir>\fR
Run in <dir>.
.TP 12n
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print this help.
.PD
.SH EXIT STATUS
.PD 0
.TP 4n
.B 0
Success, also when there was nothing to do.
.TP
.B 1
An error: no backup source here, an id that names no archive, a
passphrase needed with no terminal to ask on, or a wrong one.
.TP
.B 2
A wrong invocation: an unknown command or option, a missing value or
id, or ids given and piped in together.
.TP
.B 3
The
.I .archivectl
lacks a variable the command needs.
.PD
.PP
Any other status is borg's.
In
.BR create ,
borg's warnings, 1 and 100 to 127, keep the archive without the files
borg could not read, which it names, and the status is 0.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BORG_BACKUP_ROOT
Where
.B init
puts repositories, here or on another host; default
.IR /data/backup/borg ,
or
.I ~/.borg_backups
when
.I /data
is not mounted.
.TP
.BR BORG_PASSPHRASE ", " BORG_PASSCOMMAND
The passphrase, or a command that prints it; without either, here or in
.IR .archivectl ,
a command asks for it once.
.TP
.B BORG_RSH
What borg runs instead of ssh;
.B init
keeps it in
.IR .archivectl .
.TP
.B XDG_STATE_HOME
Where logs and archive numbers go; default
.IR ~/.local/state .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I .archivectl
The configuration of a backup source, in its directory; see
.BR archivectl (5).
.TP
.I $BORG_BACKUP_ROOT/<name>/
The repository of a source, its archives named
.IR <N>_<YYYY\-MM\-DD_HHMMSS> .
.I <name>
is the source's path with its leading
.B /
left out and every other one written
.BR __ :
.I /home/adhd/test
is kept in
.IR home__adhd__test .
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/archivectl/<name>.log
.TQ
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/archivectl/<repo>.sequence
What every writing command did, readable by you only, and the last
number in the repository, <repo> written as <name> is.
.TP
.I $PASSWORD_STORE_DIR/archivectl/<name>.gpg
The passphrase of a repository, as
.B pass show archivectl/<name>
prints it;
.B PASSWORD_STORE_DIR
defaults to
.IR ~/.password\-store .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR archivectl (5),
.BR borg (1),
.BR pass (1),
.BR snapshotctl (1)
